=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that IT Department host it_hod (192.168.1.2) cannot reach
CS Department hosts (192.168.2.0/24) — does not exist in the live lab. Direct
probes confirm full reachability: pings from it_hod to cs_hod (192.168.2.2),
cs_lab1 (192.168.2.3), and cs_lab4 (192.168.2.6) all complete with 0% packet
loss and a TTL of 63, confirming traffic is correctly being routed through
router0.

Router0's interface configuration is sound: eth0 carries 192.168.1.1/24 for the
IT LAN and eth1 carries 192.168.2.1/24 for the CS LAN, both UP and with kernel
routes installed. The RIP database on router0 lists both subnets as C(i)
(connected, interface-sourced), and they appear in the FIB as directly connected
routes. No iptables FORWARD rules exist on router0 that could drop inter-
department traffic. End-host gateway configuration is also correct: it_hod points
to 192.168.1.1 and cs_hod points to 192.168.2.1. Router1 and router2 carry
consistent RIP-learned routes back to both subnets, so the wider topology is
coherent as well.

Root cause: There is no fault — the ticket premise is false and the inter-
department path between IT (192.168.1.0/24) and CS (192.168.2.0/24) via router0
is fully operational.

Corrective action: No change is required. The helpdesk ticket should be closed as
a false alarm; if the user experienced a transient issue it may have been a
momentary lab startup delay before RIP converged, not a configuration fault.
=== END ===
